Here's the thing — you don't need the PID to answer R1. His actual objection was "how much better is it than a well-designed mode-switching controller?" The substance is: does learned thrust modulation beat not modulating? A hand-tuned PID is one way to ask that, and it's the expensive way, because it needs a human to tune it and it has to survive sim-to-real.

The cheap way is a training run: constant-thrust hybrid. Propellers held at a fixed value, wheels and servos learned normally. That's the non-modulating alternative — and it requires zero tuning, because there's nothing to tune. It's optimized by the same algorithm on the same task, so nobody can say you crippled it.

Against that, your comparison becomes:


learned modulation      the policy
fixed thrust            constant-thrust hybrid   <- replaces the PID
no thrust               wheels-only
thrust only             propeller-only
energy weight sweep     the Pareto front
That set answers R1's substance better than the PID would have, because every entry is trained by the same optimizer rather than tuned by a tired human at 2am. And "we compare against a non-modulating fixed-allocation controller" is a cleaner sentence than "we compare against a controller we retuned."

